that have been rejected or were deemed

249
00:14:16.284 --> 00:14:20.004
ineligible to apply for emergency housing?

250
00:14:20.764 --> 00:14:20.903
And-

251
00:14:20.924 --> 00:14:22.554
Yeah, that-- Yeah.

252
00:14:22.584 --> 00:14:23.844
Oh yeah, you go with that question first.

253
00:14:23.884 --> 00:14:24.294
I do have a second-

254
00:14:24.404 --> 00:14:26.684
Yeah. So look, that is a

255
00:14:27.484 --> 00:14:31.064
system issue, denial of service and the constraint of

256
00:14:31.124 --> 00:14:33.204
access, as you will have noted from the data.

257
00:14:34.024 --> 00:14:37.804
The government target to reduce emergency housing down to 800 has

258
00:14:38.244 --> 00:14:38.604
occurred

259
00:14:39.444 --> 00:14:41.104
three or four years ahead of schedule,

260
00:14:42.244 --> 00:14:46.004
and is clearly a driver of what we're seeing on our streets,

261
00:14:46.144 --> 00:14:48.044
as people don't have an alternative

262
00:14:48.964 --> 00:14:51.964
place to go. That's the place of last resort.

263
00:14:53.144 --> 00:14:57.024
And just a second question, which is

264
00:14:57.144 --> 00:14:57.604
on--

265
00:14:59.024 --> 00:15:02.924
I'm assuming that there is capacity issues currently in terms of the services

266
00:15:02.964 --> 00:15:06.664
that you provide, and there are wait lists. We've heard that from Housing First.

267
00:15:08.324 --> 00:15:08.544
What

268
00:15:09.684 --> 00:15:13.514
is the increase in the wait list now for some of the things

269
00:15:13.544 --> 00:15:14.084
that... Yeah.

270
00:15:15.104 --> 00:15:15.234
Well,

271
00:15:16.144 --> 00:15:17.844
we've had to resort,

272
00:15:18.904 --> 00:15:21.364
Laurence, to collecting our own data now,

273
00:15:22.384 --> 00:15:25.604
effectively. Because if you think of the

274
00:15:25.704 --> 00:15:28.924
MSD-administered Social Housing Register, it's an

275
00:15:28.984 --> 00:15:32.844
extraordinarily inaccurate and blunt instrument, which effectively

276
00:15:32.904 --> 00:15:36.804
tells you very little about the quantity of persistent unmet housing need in

277
00:15:36.844 --> 00:15:40.464
the system. All it really tells you is that somebody's managed to navigate their

278
00:15:40.504 --> 00:15:44.484
way into an MSD office and has managed to get their name on a list

279
00:15:44.924 --> 00:15:47.253
that is held by MSD. What we are seeing,

280
00:15:48.064 --> 00:15:51.984
by the way, our 26,000 homes across the country, they

281
00:15:52.004 --> 00:15:53.064
are all full of people.

282
00:15:53.944 --> 00:15:57.844
So it's not as if we have spare vacant capacity in our system

283
00:15:57.884 --> 00:16:01.824
to accommodate rough sleepers. Fundamental issue is that we have a

284
00:16:01.924 --> 00:16:05.584
lack of affordable and social housing in New Zealand, and until we

285
00:16:05.664 --> 00:16:07.644
address that root cause,

286
00:16:08.464 --> 00:16:10.934
I'm afraid things are going to continue to get worse, not better.

287
00:16:14.784 --> 00:16:15.104
Thank you.

288
00:16:15.364 --> 00:16:17.494
Okay. Thanks, Laurence. Any other questions?

289
00:16:18.534 --> 00:16:20.184
Doesn't appear to be the case. Thank you, Paul.

290
00:16:20.244 --> 00:16:21.864
Really appreciate you joining us this morning.

291
00:16:21.874 --> 00:16:24.344
Thank you for the work you're doing as a select committee. Really appreciate it.

292
00:16:24.904 --> 00:16:25.304
Thank you.

293
00:16:27.204 --> 00:16:28.924
All right, members, we're coming to Just Speak now.

294
00:16:28.964 --> 00:16:32.043
We've got Katie and Charles, who are both going to be joining us.

295
00:16:33.064 --> 00:16:36.664
And I can see Katie is there and Charles is just coming online, which is

296
00:16:36.684 --> 00:16:40.404
great. We have read your submission. We've got you down for 10 minutes.

297
00:16:40.534 --> 00:16:43.344
If you can just leave some time at the end for some questions as well, we'd really

298
00:16:43.354 --> 00:16:44.804
appreciate that. But over to you.

299
00:16:47.504 --> 00:16:51.054
See, call Katie Perkins talking about call Charles Harvey turn up,

300
00:16:51.444 --> 00:16:53.684
and we are board members of the kaupapa Just Speak.

301
00:16:53.844 --> 00:16:57.264
We also speak to you as a former police officer and criminal barrister.

302
00:16:57.624 --> 00:16:59.444
Thank you for the opportunity to present today.

303
00:17:01.404 --> 00:17:04.704
Offences Move On Orders Amendment Bill, and request that it be

304
00:17:04.764 --> 00:17:05.684
rejected.

305
00:17:07.684 --> 00:17:11.565
Being at the heart of our justice system by advocating for a public justice sector

306
00:17:11.664 --> 00:17:14.604
that prioritizes prevention and healing and legislation-

307
00:17:18.484 --> 00:17:20.585
I might just stop you just for a moment, Katie. Sorry.

308
00:17:20.684 --> 00:17:24.325
Your connection just seems to be dropping a little bit in and out, so we catch some

309
00:17:24.364 --> 00:17:27.664
of your first words and then don't catch the rest of your sentence, and I would

310
00:17:27.724 --> 00:17:29.964
hate for that to be your submission.

311
00:17:30.004 --> 00:17:30.704
So

312
00:17:32.384 --> 00:17:33.584
we can't hear you now.

313
00:17:36.364 --> 00:17:38.064
I can see you are talking but can't hear you.

314
00:17:39.964 --> 00:17:40.704
Is that any better?

315
00:17:41.064 --> 00:17:42.004
That is heaps better.

316
00:17:42.184 --> 00:17:42.904
Whoa, okay.

317
00:17:43.084 --> 00:17:43.274
Much better.

318
00:17:43.274 --> 00:17:43.444
All right.

319
00:17:43.494 --> 00:17:44.344
Thank you. Carry on.

320
00:17:45.484 --> 00:17:48.574
So Just Speak's mission is to put social wellbeing at the heart of our justice

321
00:17:48.644 --> 00:17:52.594
system by advocating for a public justice sector that prioritizes prevention

322
00:17:52.594 --> 00:17:55.764
and healing and legislation and policy which is based on good evidence.

323
00:17:56.344 --> 00:17:59.124
We've spent some time reflecting on what this bill is really about.

324
00:17:59.584 --> 00:18:03.064
On the surface, it is framed as a response to disorder and concerns about public

325
00:18:03.144 --> 00:18:06.804
spaces. But underneath that, we think it raises a much bigger question

326
00:18:06.924 --> 00:18:10.844
about who we are as a country and how we choose to respond to people who

327
00:18:10.864 --> 00:18:14.672
are struggling. Because legislation does more than create powers, it

328
00:18:14.712 --> 00:18:18.672
reflects values. It communicates what we believe our obligations are to one

329
00:18:18.692 --> 00:18:22.622
another. And what concerns us most about this bill is the way it encourages us to

330
00:18:22.622 --> 00:18:26.272
think about people experiencing homelessness and vulnerability.

331
00:18:26.312 --> 00:18:30.162
When we see someone sleeping rough, begging, or obviously struggling, we have

332
00:18:30.212 --> 00:18:33.992
two choices. We can ask what support they need, or we can

333
00:18:34.072 --> 00:18:37.662
ask how to make them less visible. This bill chooses visibility as the

334
00:18:37.732 --> 00:18:41.402
problem. It focuses on moving people elsewhere rather than addressing the

335
00:18:41.412 --> 00:18:43.772
circumstances that brought them there in the first place.

336
00:18:44.651 --> 00:18:48.302
It risks normalizing the idea that when people are in difficult

337
00:18:48.332 --> 00:18:52.252
circumstances, our responsibility is not to stand alongside them, but simply

338
00:18:52.292 --> 00:18:56.252
to move them on. That if hardship makes us uncomfortable, it is the

339
00:18:56.292 --> 00:18:59.812
person experiencing that hardship who should be required to move.

340
00:18:59.852 --> 00:19:02.152
We do not believe that reflects the best of Aotearoa.

341
00:19:02.792 --> 00:19:05.112
We want to be clear that homelessness is complex.

342
00:19:05.772 --> 00:19:08.732
Some people experiencing homelessness will engage with support services

343
00:19:08.772 --> 00:19:12.572
immediately, others will not. There are many reasons for that: a lack of

344
00:19:12.612 --> 00:19:16.112
trust, previous negative experiences, mental health challenges,

345
00:19:16.252 --> 00:19:20.232
addiction, past interactions with state agencies, or simply

346
00:19:20.432 --> 00:19:24.272
exercising their own autonomy and making a choice we may not understand.

347
00:19:25.052 --> 00:19:28.632
The right to make decisions about your own life does not disappear when you become

348
00:19:28.692 --> 00:19:32.672
homeless. People retain their dignity, their autonomy, and

349
00:19:32.692 --> 00:19:35.172
their mana regardless of their housing circumstances.

350
00:19:35.672 --> 00:19:38.332
And where support is being declined, we think our response

351
00:19:39.272 --> 00:19:41.152
should be curiosity rather than punishment.

352
00:19:41.482 --> 00:19:44.832
We should be asking why someone would prefer remaining on the street to engaging

353
00:19:44.892 --> 00:19:48.872
with available services. What has happened that trust has broken down to that

354
00:19:48.912 --> 00:19:49.172
point?

355
00:19:50.012 --> 00:19:52.612
What can we do differently, and what barriers exist?

356
00:19:53.392 --> 00:19:57.292
Because those questions lead us towards solutions, move on orders

357
00:19:57.352 --> 00:20:01.022
do not. Homelessness is a social issue, not a criminal

358
00:20:01.032 --> 00:20:04.772
behavior issue. There is nothing inherently criminal about being homeless.

359
00:20:05.152 --> 00:20:09.132
What this bill does is change that, and that reflects on us as a nation

360
00:20:09.292 --> 00:20:11.532
about how we view and care for our most vulnerable.

361
00:20:13.852 --> 00:20:16.972
Thank you, Katie. Kia ora koutou. I did have a few tech issues, so if you can't

362
00:20:17.012 --> 00:20:20.612
hear me, please let me know. Members,

363
00:20:21.091 --> 00:20:23.941
where JustSpeak really falls on this is that we ask the committee to look at what

364
00:20:23.952 --> 00:20:26.232
the evidence tells us then about what does work.

365
00:20:26.332 --> 00:20:30.161
And we do know that stable housing works, adequate income support works,

366
00:20:30.692 --> 00:20:34.452
wraparound mental health services, addiction treatment, recovery services,

367
00:20:35.152 --> 00:20:38.892
and long-term relationship-based support, we know works.

368
00:20:39.992 --> 00:20:43.012
These are approaches that have consistently delivered positive outcomes for our

369
00:20:43.052 --> 00:20:43.772
most vulnerable.

370
00:20:44.692 --> 00:20:48.112
By contrast, the evidence shows that criminalization of

371
00:20:48.172 --> 00:20:50.532
homelessness does not solve homelessness.

372
00:20:51.272 --> 00:20:54.052
Overseas experience and studies demonstrate that.

373
00:20:54.292 --> 00:20:57.721
If you had a chance to read our written submissions, a recent American study that

374
00:20:57.752 --> 00:21:01.592
we've cited there says that move on orders and citations

375
00:21:01.602 --> 00:21:05.372
fail to reduce urban disorder, but create instead a spatial

376
00:21:05.432 --> 00:21:09.212
churn in which homeless people circulate between neighborhoods and police

377
00:21:09.272 --> 00:21:11.932
jurisdictions rather than leaving public space.

378
00:21:12.532 --> 00:21:15.312
And the authors of that study found that these types of approaches actually

379
00:21:15.372 --> 00:21:16.932
reproduce homelessness.

380
00:21:18.632 --> 00:21:22.452
Studies examining move on powers and criminalization of homelessness have found

381
00:21:22.472 --> 00:21:26.232
that people are not removed, they're simply displaced from one location to another.

382
00:21:26.712 --> 00:21:30.452
And so the underlying homelessness remains, the poverty that's caused that remains,

383
00:21:31.012 --> 00:21:33.112
and the underlying social issues also remain.

384
00:21:33.432 --> 00:21:36.812
The only thing that does change is where those people are seen within our society.

385
00:21:38.192 --> 00:21:41.782
And so that brings me to where our real concern is about this

386
00:21:42.272 --> 00:21:44.812
bill, and that is the criminalization of homelessness.

387
00:21:44.972 --> 00:21:48.452
And a person sleeping rough, of course, is not

388
00:21:48.852 --> 00:21:51.152
committing homelessness, they're experiencing it.

389
00:21:51.292 --> 00:21:51.552
And

390
00:21:52.492 --> 00:21:56.152
should this bill be passed, all it does, in our view, is create another pathway

391
00:21:56.192 --> 00:21:59.892
into the justice system for individuals whose primary issue

392
00:21:59.932 --> 00:22:03.632
is issues of poverty, of housing insecurity, trauma,

393
00:22:03.692 --> 00:22:05.501
addiction, and disconnection.

394
00:22:06.652 --> 00:22:10.432
And we know that contact with the justice system itself can be harmful for people.

395
00:22:11.032 --> 00:22:14.702
Further research that we've cited shows that the early

396
00:22:14.712 --> 00:22:17.842
engagement with the justice system increases the likelihood of

397
00:22:17.872 --> 00:22:21.322
re-offending and can steer people away from pro-social pathways and

398
00:22:21.392 --> 00:22:25.192
opportunities. And once people become involved with the justice system, it becomes

399
00:22:25.232 --> 00:22:27.192
harder to secure employment, housing,

400
00:22:28.012 --> 00:22:29.712
and their lifestyle becomes unstable.

401
00:22:30.312 --> 00:22:33.812
And so for people who are already struggling, this can be another barrier that they

402
00:22:33.872 --> 00:22:34.732
carry for years.

403
00:22:36.342 --> 00:22:39.592
There are also some practical concerns that JustSpeak has.

404
00:22:40.712 --> 00:22:40.822
The

405
00:22:42.072 --> 00:22:44.072
offenses under this amendment

406
00:22:45.052 --> 00:22:47.432
carry a maximum penalty of three months imprisonment.

407
00:22:48.312 --> 00:22:51.962
And we know that once someone enters the criminal justice system, the consequences

408
00:22:51.992 --> 00:22:54.732
can extend far beyond the statutory maximum penalty.

409
00:22:55.352 --> 00:22:59.252
So for example, those who we see regularly before the courts with long conviction

410
00:22:59.272 --> 00:23:02.872
histories and with difficulties with being granted bail

411
00:23:03.352 --> 00:23:07.222
are at risk of having remands in custody longer than the statutory maximum that

412
00:23:07.272 --> 00:23:10.612
they'd receive for offenses under the act.

413
00:23:10.652 --> 00:23:14.192
So that process itself can become a punishment, and it all stems from

414
00:23:14.232 --> 00:23:17.652
legislation that's directed at people whose underlying issue is that they have

415
00:23:17.692 --> 00:23:21.412
nowhere else to go. And so JustSpeak also wants to

416
00:23:21.452 --> 00:23:23.792
acknowledge the impact that this bill would have on young people.

417
00:23:24.092 --> 00:23:25.352
The bill extends these powers,

418
00:23:26.612 --> 00:23:29.772
orders to be imposed on people as young as 14.

419
00:23:30.852 --> 00:23:34.192
Manaaki Rangatahi: Responding to youth homelessness in Aotearoa New Zealand, a

420
00:23:34.232 --> 00:23:38.022
recent published Maori-led homelessness research paper, speaks about

421
00:23:38.032 --> 00:23:41.882
rangatahi as taonga, deserving protection, dignity, connection,

422
00:23:41.992 --> 00:23:45.872
and care. And we think that principle is important because it extends

423
00:23:45.912 --> 00:23:49.662
beyond rangatahi, because people experiencing homelessness are not

424
00:23:49.672 --> 00:23:53.492
problems that need to be managed. They're people with mana, with dignity,

425
00:23:54.192 --> 00:23:55.392
and they're members of our community.

426
00:23:56.992 --> 00:23:57.682
And so finally,

427
00:23:59.532 --> 00:24:02.742
again, from a practical perspective, we note that, and I think the submission has

428
00:24:02.752 --> 00:24:05.512
been made to this committee earlier in this week, is that the

429
00:24:07.472 --> 00:24:11.372
issues that this bill is seeking to address can already be adequately

430
00:24:11.432 --> 00:24:13.132
addressed under the Summary Offenses Act.

431
00:24:14.768 --> 00:24:18.448
So issues of disorderly conduct, violence, intimidation,

432
00:24:18.848 --> 00:24:19.988
blocking public ways

433
00:24:21.148 --> 00:24:23.808
are already addressed within the Summary Offences Act.

434
00:24:23.828 --> 00:24:26.948
And so the proposed Section 8A in the bill

435
00:24:28.028 --> 00:24:31.928
only really goes to extend criminalization to paragraphs

436
00:24:34.368 --> 00:24:38.288
E and F, which involve the conduct of begging and rough sleeping, which

437
00:24:38.348 --> 00:24:42.118
are hallmarks of homelessness. So in our submission, that this bill,

438
00:24:42.188 --> 00:24:46.008
all it does is extend the criminal liability scope

439
00:24:46.248 --> 00:24:46.888
to include

440
00:24:47.888 --> 00:24:50.088
homeless, well, symptoms of homelessness.

441
00:24:50.948 --> 00:24:54.818
So then the question really is, how do we deal with harmful behavior?

442
00:24:54.837 --> 00:24:58.328
And we suggest that that can already be dealt with under the Summary Offences Act.

443
00:24:59.228 --> 00:25:03.028
So whether homelessness itself should be a trigger for police

444
00:25:03.068 --> 00:25:06.468
intervention, and the criminal justice involvement, we say

445
00:25:07.008 --> 00:25:08.188
that shouldn't be the case.

446
00:25:09.008 --> 00:25:11.008
So we say that as a values question,

447
00:25:12.128 --> 00:25:13.868
how do we deal with our most vulnerable?

448
00:25:14.368 --> 00:25:17.928
Do we offer a hand up or do we encourage people to simply move somewhere else so

449
00:25:17.968 --> 00:25:19.128
that we can't see them?

450
00:25:20.508 --> 00:25:24.448
And so with those values of dignity, care, respect, and upholding

451
00:25:24.458 --> 00:25:28.438
people's mana, we say that the committee should reject this bill in its

452
00:25:28.488 --> 00:25:31.768
entirety, and we're grateful for the opportunity to speak to you today.

453
00:25:31.788 --> 00:25:35.708
Fantastic. Thank you, Charles, and thank you, Katie.

454
00:25:35.788 --> 00:25:37.708
We do have a question from Dr. Duncan Webb.

455
00:25:38.748 --> 00:25:42.268
Kia ora, thank you for that. Look, I'm particularly

456
00:25:42.288 --> 00:25:46.148
interested in what an appropriate response might be,

457
00:25:46.528 --> 00:25:50.178
not so much in terms of prevention,

458
00:25:50.428 --> 00:25:54.288
because we all want to do that. But there's been some suggestion

459
00:25:54.328 --> 00:25:58.188
that these are tools that the police need, and you've got some police

460
00:25:58.248 --> 00:26:02.068
experience there. If you're dealing with people that are

461
00:26:02.668 --> 00:26:06.208
creating a little bit of a public nuisance but perhaps not

462
00:26:07.728 --> 00:26:11.688
committing an offense, what might an appropriate policing

463
00:26:11.728 --> 00:26:13.008
response look like?

464
00:26:15.738 --> 00:26:17.008
Thank you for the question. Well, I think

465
00:26:18.748 --> 00:26:22.288
it may be practically that the police are the first to have that

466
00:26:22.328 --> 00:26:26.078
contact. And I think you're right that those sort of, I guess,

467
00:26:26.168 --> 00:26:29.008
gray areas between sort of genuine

468
00:26:29.888 --> 00:26:33.368
pro-social public behavior and conduct that would otherwise be criminal.

469
00:26:34.178 --> 00:26:35.618
The gray zone in the middle is

470
00:26:36.928 --> 00:26:40.388
the hardest to deal with. And you're right, the police are practically likely to be

471
00:26:40.408 --> 00:26:42.398
the first ones to engage with those individuals.

472
00:26:43.678 --> 00:26:46.228
There's essentially a triage and referral approach.

473
00:26:47.208 --> 00:26:50.268
Those people need to then be engaged with other social support,

474
00:26:51.048 --> 00:26:52.988
and ultimately have somewhere else to go.

475
00:26:52.998 --> 00:26:56.908
And I appreciate that that type of conduct in public spaces can be

476
00:26:56.948 --> 00:26:59.628
difficult, and can be unsettling for some people.

477
00:27:00.908 --> 00:27:03.868
But our concern with the bill is that

478
00:27:04.908 --> 00:27:08.288
more of that gray zone is now shifting towards the criminalization.

479
00:27:08.708 --> 00:27:09.028
And

480
00:27:10.548 --> 00:27:13.568
it does come down to a resourcing of

481
00:27:14.028 --> 00:27:17.448
agencies being able to intervene at that point to assist these people.

482
00:27:17.528 --> 00:27:19.598
I mean, if they engage in conduct that

483
00:27:20.428 --> 00:27:23.268
may not be pro-social, but not criminal and just generally disruptive,

484
00:27:24.418 --> 00:27:25.998
there's likely to be further social,

485
00:27:26.868 --> 00:27:30.428
mental health, physical issues going on that need to be addressed.

486
00:27:31.248 --> 00:27:32.778
The police are not the ones adequately

487
00:27:33.728 --> 00:27:37.628
resourced or trained to deal with that, and so it is a referral and

488
00:27:37.688 --> 00:27:40.708
triage approach that Justice Access would be the appropriate

489
00:27:41.668 --> 00:27:42.228
pathway there.

490
00:27:44.268 --> 00:27:46.368
