But the move on orders are mainly being applied into some of our cities, isn't it,

3448
03:24:19.690 --> 03:24:23.510
rather than papakāinga. In papakāinga, you're talking about mainly in

3449
03:24:23.950 --> 03:24:27.470
rural areas. But the move on orders are really

3450
03:24:27.510 --> 03:24:31.290
attacking so people are not visible anymore. Is that correct?

3451
03:24:31.830 --> 03:24:33.070
Not necessarily attacking,

3452
03:24:33.950 --> 03:24:37.390
doctor. With papakāinga, it's one of the things that's available

3453
03:24:38.270 --> 03:24:40.450
as what we're doing because you were saying there's no strategy.

3454
03:24:40.530 --> 03:24:43.350
But with the move on orders, it's 24 hours.

3455
03:24:43.750 --> 03:24:47.450
It's a response to as well people that have families, that are running

3456
03:24:47.530 --> 03:24:51.390
businesses, that are saying, "This is causing a problem for us as well

3457
03:24:51.450 --> 03:24:55.090
to pay our mortgage and send our kids to school." So it's

3458
03:24:55.350 --> 03:24:59.170
a whole approach. It doesn't deny the fact that there's issues for our

3459
03:24:59.550 --> 03:25:02.810
homeless people. That as well, we've got a minister for mental health.

3460
03:25:03.150 --> 03:25:04.830
He's doing a lot in that area as well.

3461
03:25:05.370 --> 03:25:07.530
Can you answer me a question there?

3462
03:25:07.570 --> 03:25:11.290
Where can I find that information about the number of complaints you've had from

3463
03:25:11.350 --> 03:25:12.170
business owners?

3464
03:25:13.290 --> 03:25:14.410
I don't have the number

3465
03:25:15.310 --> 03:25:18.130
here, but I know what I hear in South Auckland as a local community-

3466
03:25:18.150 --> 03:25:21.950
Yeah, I don't want to hear anecdote. I want to know what's your evidence.

3467
03:25:22.250 --> 03:25:23.560
Anecdote's evidence for me.

3468
03:25:23.560 --> 03:25:27.510
To be-- And I'm not undermining or trying to minimize what

3469
03:25:27.670 --> 03:25:31.230
business owners are saying to you, but I'm hearing a lot from this

3470
03:25:31.270 --> 03:25:34.970
government about somebody said, and I really am interested in the

3471
03:25:35.050 --> 03:25:38.570
evidence that underpins the decision to have this bill.

3472
03:25:39.170 --> 03:25:42.690
Fair enough, doctor. We've spoken to a lot of people that run

3473
03:25:42.750 --> 03:25:44.030
businesses that have families.

3474
03:25:44.510 --> 03:25:47.620
Yeah. It'd be good to see where the information is, though. Kia ora.

3475
03:25:48.030 --> 03:25:49.520
We'll go to a question from Dan Rosewarne.

3476
03:25:49.750 --> 03:25:51.450
Kia ora, Dan.

3477
03:25:51.630 --> 03:25:53.110
Yeah. Thanks for that submission.

3478
03:25:53.430 --> 03:25:56.850
You highlighted in the submission that some people remain in

3479
03:25:56.970 --> 03:26:00.090
busy public places because they feel safer there.

3480
03:26:00.720 --> 03:26:04.530
What do you think could happen if those people are moved away

3481
03:26:04.610 --> 03:26:08.310
from places where they are visible into areas where

3482
03:26:08.350 --> 03:26:09.730
fewer people can see them?

3483
03:26:11.330 --> 03:26:12.090
I think there's a

3484
03:26:13.190 --> 03:26:17.070
bigger chance of them being exposed to

3485
03:26:17.110 --> 03:26:21.010
violence or abuse. That's why I think they place themselves

3486
03:26:21.070 --> 03:26:21.250
there,

3487
03:26:22.290 --> 03:26:25.690
is because they are lit up streets. There's people walking by.

3488
03:26:26.930 --> 03:26:27.710
They're visible.

3489
03:26:28.590 --> 03:26:32.430
If they have to start to move, which street are they going to move to

3490
03:26:32.570 --> 03:26:34.390
if they have to move from that street they're in?

3491
03:26:35.410 --> 03:26:39.010
They're homeless, full stop. So if they have to

3492
03:26:39.090 --> 03:26:42.750
move, then whose street do we want them to move into?

3493
03:26:43.110 --> 03:26:47.030
Or do we want them to go off streets and

3494
03:26:47.130 --> 03:26:49.230
somewhere else? That's what

3495
03:26:50.430 --> 03:26:53.770
is a big concern because we do have a lot of women.

3496
03:26:54.470 --> 03:26:58.110
We work closely with Women's Refuge, who are also seeing a lot of

3497
03:26:58.150 --> 03:27:00.870
homeless who are not just women, but elderly

3498
03:27:04.094 --> 03:27:04.814
Kia ora. Thank you.

3499
03:27:05.814 --> 03:27:08.854
Thank you, Hope. Really appreciate you joining us this morning.

3500
03:27:09.074 --> 03:27:09.274
Thank you.

3501
03:27:09.284 --> 03:27:10.353
And thank you for your written submission.

3502
03:27:12.934 --> 03:27:16.054
Okay, we're going to come to the Wellington Community Justice Project, Howard

3503
03:27:16.134 --> 03:27:18.854
League submissions team, with Gemma and Mai.

3504
03:27:22.894 --> 03:27:25.434
Hey, Gemma. We can see you nice and clearly.

3505
03:27:25.494 --> 03:27:27.514
Mai's just coming online now.

3506
03:27:29.634 --> 03:27:32.374
And there you are as well. Fantastic. Thank you both for joining us this morning.

3507
03:27:32.414 --> 03:27:35.434
We've got you down for 10 minutes. We've read your submission, if you can just

3508
03:27:35.474 --> 03:27:36.974
leave some time at the end for questions.

3509
03:27:37.594 --> 03:27:41.574
Awesome. Kia ora. We are Mai Khalid and Gemma Benin,

3510
03:27:42.134 --> 03:27:45.514
law students, volunteers from the Wellington Community Justice Project,

3511
03:27:45.624 --> 03:27:49.454
specifically the Howard League submissions team, which advocates for penal

3512
03:27:49.514 --> 03:27:53.504
reform and criminal justice. While we are supported by the Howard League,

3513
03:27:53.674 --> 03:27:56.414
the opinions expressed in this submission are our own.

3514
03:27:56.914 --> 03:28:00.134
We are opposed to the Move on Orders Bill for the following reasons.

3515
03:28:01.994 --> 03:28:05.834
I will be addressing two issues. First, why we submit the move on

3516
03:28:05.894 --> 03:28:09.394
orders are inconsistent with fundamental rights and Te Tiriti

3517
03:28:09.454 --> 03:28:13.394
obligations. Secondly, why we submit they are likely to increase

3518
03:28:13.474 --> 03:28:17.034
incarceration while placing New Zealand at odds with its international

3519
03:28:17.114 --> 03:28:17.894
obligations.

3520
03:28:18.794 --> 03:28:22.414
Turning first to rights, our concern is that this bill

3521
03:28:22.494 --> 03:28:26.054
gives police broad discretion to issue a move on order where they

3522
03:28:26.064 --> 03:28:29.614
suspect someone has behaved in a disorderly or disruptive

3523
03:28:29.674 --> 03:28:33.354
manner. Those concepts are broad, subjective, and open

3524
03:28:33.414 --> 03:28:37.134
to inconsistent application. We submit that where

3525
03:28:37.174 --> 03:28:41.014
Parliament grants powers that interfere with fundamental rights, they must be

3526
03:28:41.114 --> 03:28:44.394
clearly defined, proportionate, and demonstrably justified.

3527
03:28:45.154 --> 03:28:48.894
These concerns are further reinforced when viewed through the lens of Te

3528
03:28:48.974 --> 03:28:49.994
Tiriti o Waitangi.

3529
03:28:50.874 --> 03:28:54.764
Maori continue to experience disproportionately high rates of homelessness and

3530
03:28:54.854 --> 03:28:58.844
severe housing deprivation. We are particularly concerned about

3531
03:28:58.844 --> 03:29:02.834
the impact on rangatahi, as the bill applies to young people aged 14

3532
03:29:02.854 --> 03:29:03.204
or over.

3533
03:29:04.114 --> 03:29:07.334
For a young person sleeping rough, a move on order does not resolve the

3534
03:29:07.374 --> 03:29:09.314
circumstances that brought them there.

3535
03:29:09.374 --> 03:29:13.274
Instead, it may require them to leave an area where they feel relatively safe

3536
03:29:13.314 --> 03:29:16.314
or risk breaching the order and entering the criminal justice system.

3537
03:29:17.054 --> 03:29:20.954
We submit that this disproportionate impact engages the Crown's obligations

3538
03:29:20.994 --> 03:29:23.594
of partnership and active protection under Te Tiriti.

3539
03:29:24.214 --> 03:29:28.134
And turning briefly to our second point, our written submission explains why

3540
03:29:28.194 --> 03:29:30.994
we are concerned that the bill will increase incarceration.

3541
03:29:32.374 --> 03:29:35.854
We would emphasize that this is not because the bill responds to conduct

3542
03:29:35.914 --> 03:29:39.214
that police currently cannot address.

3543
03:29:39.934 --> 03:29:43.594
Existing provisions under the Summary Offences Act already

3544
03:29:43.634 --> 03:29:47.194
provide police with powers to respond to genuinely threatening or disorderly

3545
03:29:47.294 --> 03:29:51.154
behavior. Instead, the bill creates a new offense for breaching a move on

3546
03:29:51.234 --> 03:29:53.644
order, carrying the possibility of imprisonment.

3547
03:29:54.294 --> 03:29:57.634
We therefore question whether this additional offense is either necessary or

3548
03:29:57.674 --> 03:30:01.574
proportionate. Finally, we submit that these concerns

3549
03:30:01.614 --> 03:30:04.624
extend beyond domestic law. New Zealand has

3550
03:30:04.674 --> 03:30:08.274
longstanding commitments under international human rights

3551
03:30:08.354 --> 03:30:11.924
instruments, including the International Covenant on Civil and

3552
03:30:12.014 --> 03:30:15.934
Political Rights. Those commitments reinforce the importance of protecting

3553
03:30:16.054 --> 03:30:19.974
freedom of movement, expression, and peaceful assembly while ensuring imprisonment

3554
03:30:20.034 --> 03:30:23.503
is used consistently with human dignity and only where justified.

3555
03:30:24.014 --> 03:30:27.414
Ultimately, we submit that the bill expands the reach of the criminal law without

3556
03:30:27.424 --> 03:30:31.114
addressing the circumstances that give rise to the conduct it seeks to regulate.

3557
03:30:31.594 --> 03:30:35.314
For those reasons, we respectfully ask the committee to recommend that the bill not

3558
03:30:35.334 --> 03:30:38.994
proceed. Ngā mihi nui. I will now pass on to Gemma.

3559
03:30:40.974 --> 03:30:41.374
Kia ora.

3560
03:30:42.554 --> 03:30:45.534
The second half of our submission, we're just going to talk about why we think

3561
03:30:46.374 --> 03:30:49.894
a better approach to the policy issue is social welfare.

3562
03:30:50.694 --> 03:30:54.174
But first, just using the Scottish Sentencing and Penal Policy Commission report

3563
03:30:54.254 --> 03:30:57.714
that was published in February, we'd like to highlight why criminalizing the issue

3564
03:30:57.754 --> 03:30:58.814
is a poor approach.

3565
03:30:59.954 --> 03:31:03.334
Scotland's like us. They have overcrowded prisons with staff stretched to their

3566
03:31:03.374 --> 03:31:07.254
limits, meaning that rehabilitative opportunities are limited and remand numbers

3567
03:31:07.274 --> 03:31:11.254
are on the rise. Considering this, their Justice Directorate recently found,

3568
03:31:11.294 --> 03:31:15.114
among other things, that having a high prison population does not

3569
03:31:15.174 --> 03:31:19.034
equate to a safer society, and that reconviction rates are actually higher for

3570
03:31:19.074 --> 03:31:20.913
those imprisoned for short sentences.

3571
03:31:21.714 --> 03:31:25.423
And finally, that general overcrowding reduces the effectiveness of rehabilitative

3572
03:31:25.514 --> 03:31:28.414
programs for people convicted of more serious offenses.

3573
03:31:29.474 --> 03:31:32.994
So, the point to draw from that is that if, as per kind order statistics,

3574
03:31:33.214 --> 03:31:36.984
6,000 Kiwis are without accommodation each night, then it's actually for the good

3575
03:31:37.014 --> 03:31:40.964
of all of us that those people first and foremost are provided a home and

3576
03:31:40.974 --> 03:31:44.954
do not go to prison. They need addiction treatment, they don't need to be arrested

3577
03:31:44.994 --> 03:31:47.294
when they mouth off to an officer who's asked them to move on.

3578
03:31:48.434 --> 03:31:51.654
And they do not need a criminal sentence, which not only stretches our criminal

3579
03:31:51.674 --> 03:31:55.534
justice system further, but is a much less efficient use of taxpayer money.

3580
03:31:56.374 --> 03:31:59.974
If the government is worried about people feeling safe, as noted by Mai, the police

3581
03:31:59.994 --> 03:32:02.024
already have powers to deal with public disorder.

3582
03:32:03.154 --> 03:32:06.374
Their comments on the earlier version of this bill did support increased powers to

3583
03:32:06.414 --> 03:32:10.294
deal with aggressive behavior specifically, but they expressly rejected the

3584
03:32:10.354 --> 03:32:11.854
idea of criminalizing homelessness.

3585
03:32:12.854 --> 03:32:15.994
As the government has chosen to ignore this, we argue therefore that the bill is

3586
03:32:16.034 --> 03:32:19.094
not about safety, but having an out of sight, out of mind approach to

3587
03:32:20.094 --> 03:32:24.014
people and giving them the right services and

3588
03:32:24.394 --> 03:32:25.684
operating the criminal justice system to achieve this.

3589
03:32:27.194 --> 03:32:30.374
In sum, we'd just like to point out that there are many ways in which Kiwis are

3590
03:32:30.414 --> 03:32:34.394
feeling unsafe at the moment, from people dying in ED waiting rooms to

3591
03:32:34.454 --> 03:32:36.174
unprecedented climate weather events.

3592
03:32:36.914 --> 03:32:39.674
We would argue that a child sleeping rough is not one of them.

3593
03:32:40.534 --> 03:32:43.354
Therefore, we implore the government to abandon this bill because we believe it

3594
03:32:43.374 --> 03:32:46.914
distracts from real issues, and it has caused so much controversy.

3595
03:32:47.354 --> 03:32:50.884
And finally, even the New Zealand Police have said that they do not support it, and

3596
03:32:50.974 --> 03:32:54.774
it will actually take away from police resourcing in other, more appropriate areas.

3597
03:32:55.494 --> 03:32:58.364
Ngā mihi nui. We thank the committee for their time, and we will now take

3598
03:32:58.394 --> 03:32:59.634
questions.

3599
03:32:59.674 --> 03:33:01.274
Thank you both for your submission.

3600
03:33:01.314 --> 03:33:03.114
We've got a question from Dan Rosewarms

3601
03:33:04.478 --> 03:33:08.318
Hey, thanks for that submission. It highlights that the

3602
03:33:08.418 --> 03:33:12.298
bill identifies a real problem but criminalizes

3603
03:33:12.358 --> 03:33:14.698
the symptom rather than addressing the cause.

3604
03:33:15.538 --> 03:33:18.958
And I suppose my question is, what do you see as the root causes of the

3605
03:33:19.038 --> 03:33:22.518
behavior that this bill is targeting, and how should

3606
03:33:22.598 --> 03:33:25.418
Parliament respond to those causes instead?

3607
03:33:27.938 --> 03:33:31.478
I'll just jump in if that's okay. I actually went to a book talk of Asher

3608
03:33:31.578 --> 03:33:34.998
Manuel's beautiful "The Valley" novel there last night, and

3609
03:33:35.918 --> 03:33:37.978
I think it's mandatory reading for absolutely everybody.

3610
03:33:38.038 --> 03:33:41.318
But that really identifies, I think, the main issues, which are

3611
03:33:41.378 --> 03:33:45.248
that housing, healthcare, education, these are all things that we know

3612
03:33:45.358 --> 03:33:49.338
prevent crime because they prevent poverty, because poverty is one of the main

3613
03:33:49.358 --> 03:33:52.838
drivers of criminal behavior. So I think, when we've seen an

3614
03:33:52.898 --> 03:33:56.858
underfunding of all those services in recent years, that it's no wonder that

3615
03:33:56.898 --> 03:33:57.938
there are more people on the streets.

3616
03:33:58.098 --> 03:34:01.878
And actually, if you want to target that issue, it's those social reforms that you

3617
03:34:01.918 --> 03:34:04.278
need to focus on rather than moving people into prison.

3618
03:34:04.358 --> 03:34:05.558
I don't know what Maia thinks about that.

3619
03:34:07.018 --> 03:34:09.318
Thank you, and thank you for your service, too.

3620
03:34:09.998 --> 03:34:10.368
Kia ora.

3621
03:34:10.918 --> 03:34:12.928
Thank you both for your time this afternoon.

3622
03:34:12.978 --> 03:34:16.958
Really appreciate you presenting to us and for your written submission as well.

3623
03:34:16.998 --> 03:34:17.438
Thank you.

3624
03:34:18.038 --> 03:34:18.358
Thank you.

3625
03:34:19.378 --> 03:34:23.178
Okay, our next submitter is going to be Kickback, and we're going to have

3626
03:34:23.258 --> 03:34:24.878
Aaron, who's going to be presenting to us.

3627
03:34:36.998 --> 03:34:39.238
Hey, Aaron, thank you for joining us this afternoon.

3628
03:34:39.418 --> 03:34:40.918
We've read your written submission.

3629
03:34:40.978 --> 03:34:43.978
We've got you down for 10 minutes, if you can just leave some time at the end for

3630
03:34:44.018 --> 03:34:45.738
questions from members. Thank you.

3631
03:34:50.398 --> 03:34:53.678
Yeah. Kia ora. Aaron Hendry, te koro. Look, I'm the general manager of Kickback.

3632
03:34:54.038 --> 03:34:57.478
We are a youth development organization that is responding to youth homelessness.

3633
03:34:58.578 --> 03:35:02.058
We run the Front Door service, which is a crisis response early intervention

3634
03:35:02.158 --> 03:35:04.418
service for children and young people.

3635
03:35:05.598 --> 03:35:08.348
Right now, we're monitoring over 200 children and young people who are either

3636
03:35:08.358 --> 03:35:10.538
experiencing homelessness or at risk of homelessness.

3637
03:35:10.658 --> 03:35:14.517
Over 20% of those over the last three months that we've been supporting

3638
03:35:14.598 --> 03:35:15.498
have been children.

3639
03:35:16.618 --> 03:35:20.218
So that's under 16. So look, we really want to highlight that this bill is a piece

3640
03:35:20.238 --> 03:35:23.198
of legislation that directly impacts the children and young people we see every

3641
03:35:23.258 --> 03:35:23.458
day.

3642
03:35:24.418 --> 03:35:27.117
We're seeing an increase of children in terms of the context of what this bill

3643
03:35:27.158 --> 03:35:30.098
comes into. We're seeing an increase of children experiencing homelessness.

3644
